{"name":"Fireside Chat","hex":"#b87427","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fireside chat","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any one of a series of 30 evening radio talks given by United States President Franklin Delano Roosevelt between 1933 and 1944.","An informal address delivered by a political leader over radio, television, or the Internet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fireside_chat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fireside chats","blocks":[{"text":"The fireside chats were a series of evening radio addresses given by Franklin D. Roosevelt, the 32nd President of the United States, between 1933 and 1944. Roosevelt spoke with familiarity to millions of Americans about recovery from the Great Depression, the promulgation of the Emergency Banking Act in response to the banking crisis, the 1937 recession, New Deal initiatives, and the course of World War II. On radio, he quelled rumors, countered conservative-dominated newspapers, and explained his policies directly to the American people. His tone and demeanor communicated self-assurance during times of despair and uncertainty. Roosevelt was regarded as an effective communicator on radio, and the fireside chats kept him in high public regard throughout his presidency. Their introduction was later described as a \"revolutionary experiment with a nascent media platform.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/FDR-March-12-1933.jpg/330px-FDR-March-12-1933.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fireside_chats"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/NBC_microphone%2C_National_Museum_of_American_History.jpg/500px-NBC_microphone%2C_National_Museum_of_American_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/NBC_microphone%2C_National_Museum_of_American_History.jpg/1280px-NBC_microphone%2C_National_Museum_of_American_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/NBC_microphone%2C_National_Museum_of_American_History.jpg/500px-NBC_microphone%2C_National_Museum_of_American_History.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"NBC microphone used for Roosevelt's fireside chat radio broadcasts","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANBC_microphone%2C_National_Museum_of_American_History.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/FDR-June-28-1934.jpg/500px-FDR-June-28-1934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/FDR-June-28-1934.jpg/960px-FDR-June-28-1934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/FDR-June-28-1934.jpg/500px-FDR-June-28-1934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFDR-June-28-1934.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/FDR-September-30-1934.jpg/330px-FDR-September-30-1934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/FDR-September-30-1934.jpg/960px-FDR-September-30-1934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/FDR-September-30-1934.jpg/330px-FDR-September-30-1934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFDR-September-30-1934.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/FDR-April-28-1935-side.jpg/330px-FDR-April-2